{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_2ff06dba90b540b68f861c28","type":"tool_result","content":"===\n18:## Batch queue\n19-\n20-| Span | Parent | State | CI | Review | PR |\n21-|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n22-| 3089-3098 | main | OPEN | PENDING | PENDING | - |\n23-\n24-## Benchmark trend\n25-\n26-| Span | OWASP Java | OWASP Python | RealVuln precision | RealVuln recall |\n27-|---|---|---|---|---|\n28-| 2000-2009 | unknown | unknown | unknown | unknown |\n===\n545:| rank 3019 | engine-gap | the engine does not model stack frame sizes or the transitive stack depth of a call chain, so a thread's declared stack size cannot be compared against the stack its entry point's call chain consumes | #489 |\n546:| rank 3046 | engine-gap | the Rust frontend records no signature or impl-header facts a binding can read -- a function's return type, its parameter and receiver types, and which trait an impl block implements are absent from the graph -- so a weakness whose vulnerable and fixed spellings differ only in types, such as an API whose items are typed by the impl's lifetime instead of the receiver borrow or an owning Iterator declaration over a lending accessor, cannot be separated at the data layer | #492 |\n547:| rank 3070 | engine-gap | taint is not carried out of a callee's write into its pointer parameter back to the caller's object, so a receive-shaped callee leaves the caller's post-call reads of the buffer it filled untainted, and the binding-declared call-effect table can only rebind caller arguments that are already tainted at the call site | #498 |\n548:| rank 3074 | engine-gap | taint is not followed through a python method call on an attribute that several distinct classes may hold, so a dispatch helper returning different provider classes loses the flow | #496 |\n549:| rank 3080 | engine-gap | a containment check a resolved callee performs over its own parameter is not attributed to the caller's function scope, so a caller that re