{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_0bba45d8138146b4a19bea93","type":"tool_result","content":"=== f24ca80320\ncommit f24ca8032054175bc4d04b2098567242e541dca2\nAuthor: Kiki Luqman Hakiem <4023108+kikihakiem@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Fri Sep 18 15:30:48 2026 +0700\n\n    triage: drift-aware false-positive suppression, anchored to the taint path (#248)\n    \n    * triage: drift-aware false-positive suppression, anchored to the taint path\n    \n    A baseline entry can now carry the finding's path signature: a digest of\n    the concepts and call paths along its witness. Such an entry suppresses\n    only while the path still hashes to one of its signatures; the same\n    fingerprint on a changed path re-fires as drifted, because a verdict\n    recorded against a path that no longer exists describes nothing.\n    \n    - resultpolicy.PathSignature: the one digest (guard extended to match);\n      findings.Finding.Sig computed at build from the store's hop labels\n    - graph-json: findings carry sig; the document carries a baseline\n      section (applied/covered/drifted/stale) so a consumer can keep records\n      alive, archive them and route re-verifications without stderr\n    - vyql triage add|remove|list: record one verdict at a time, without a\n=== db3125074d\ncommit db3125074d9fae825aed0d0e5468d667749e1cb1\nAuthor: Kiki Luqman Hakiem <4023108+kikihakiem@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Thu Sep 17 21:17:23 2026 +0700\n\n    engine: the-c-frontend-has-no-fact-for-a-nul-delimiter-terminated-st (#229)\n    \n    * Emit the string-scan missing-length-bound fact for strspn/strcspn\n    \n    CVE-2017-5209 (libplist) was blocked on a fact no C-frontend analysis\n    carried: a NUL/delimiter-terminated string scan -- strspn(3) or\n    strcspn(3), whose only stop condition is a byte value and never a count\n    -- applied to a buffer whose real bound is a separate length in the same\n    function. cursor_loop.missing_end_sentinel covers a raw\n    dereference-and-advance loop in the function's own tree and\n    pointer_offset.missing_remaining_size covers an arithmetic guard of the\n    shape ptr+len+1>end; neither matches a walk performed by an opaque libc\n    call whose bounds check (ptr >= buf+len) runs only after the unbounded\n    scan has already executed.\n    \n    analysis.string_scan.missing_length_bound pairs the two halves from the\n=== f71f7be22a\ncommit f71f7be22a319fa94ccbd1dc713276fb91d6fac5\nAuthor: Kiki Luqman Hakiem <4023108+kikihakiem@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Thu Sep 17 19:41:38 2026 +0700\n\n    engine: taint-is-not-traced-into-a-variadic-c-function-call-so-a-pri (#233)\n    \n    * c: trace a variadic call's tail arguments into the wrapper that reads them\n    \n    A C function declares its variadic tail as `...`, which has no parameter\n    name, so the arguments a call passed beyond the named parameters bound to\n    nothing: they kept only the conservative argument-to-result edge, which\n    never enters the callee. A printf-style logging wrapper is exactly that\n    shape and returns void, so every route from a caller's data to the\n    wrapper's own body — where the v-formatted output call lives — was dark,\n    and no definition could name a flow through it (rank 3007, the AIDE\n    control-character log output, CVE-2025-54389).\n    \n    The tail is now modelled the way the JavaScript frontend models\n    `arguments`. A definition that declares `...` and opens a va_list over it\n    gains a synthetic last parameter, __varargs__; the lowering binds every","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"e81e80ac-d30b-47b5-908b-85bc47c5dd66","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:13:56.762Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== f24ca80320\ncommit f24ca8032054175bc4d04b2098567242e541dca2\nAuthor: Kiki Luqman Hakiem <4023108+kikihakiem@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Fri Sep 18 15:30:48 2026 +0700\n\n  