{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_1c37b87907494406bb001fc5","type":"tool_result","content":"commit 31d5f496e3978c99ed91893c6cc388735759fe90\nAuthor: vyql bot <vyql.bot@vyprsec.ai>\nDate:   Wed Sep 16 23:10:22 2026 +0000\n\n    engine: hold a one-graph bounded scan's graph in RAM when the ceiling fits it\n    \n    -max-ram armed the disk-backed BadgerGraph store for every scan it\n    bounded. For a target whose graph fits in the ceiling, the store costs\n    more than the graph it spares: its detail buffer, write path and caches\n    sit resident alongside a structural core that never leaves RAM in\n    either store, so a multi-package repository that lowers to a\n    million-odd nodes peaked several times its in-RAM footprint (measured\n    4626MiB through the disk store against 1220MiB in RAM on the same\n    repository) and the memory safety stop ended the scan before the first\n    rule ran — an empty report from a 4GB ceiling the scan easily afforded.\n    \n    The partition planner's own affordability measure now decides the\n    store: a target PlanPartitions leaves unpartitioned is a target one\n    graph holds, and that graph is built on the int-indexed in-RAM store —\n    the same store a partitioned scan already builds per partition. A\n    graph the ceiling cannot hold still spills: a target over the\n    one-graph limit is partitioned, and a run that must serialise one\n    graph (-stats, graph-json with review flags) keeps the disk-backed\n    bound. Unbounded scans are untouched; a scan whose cache directories\n    are unwritable keeps its existing in-RAM fallback.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\ndiff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md\nindex cd1faed11f..f3d7c922d1 100644\n--- a/CHANGELOG.md\n+++ b/CHANGELOG.md\n@@ -8,6 +8,29 @@ New rules and bindings change what a scan reports, so they get listed here like\n any other user-visible change. A finding that suddenly appears in your CI is a\n behaviour change, even if no code moved.\n \n+## [Unreleased]\n+\n+### Fixed\n+\n===\ndiff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md\nindex cd1faed11f..f3d7c922d1 100644\n--- a/CHANGELOG.md\n+++ b/CHANGELOG.md\n@@ -8,6 +8,29 @@ New rules and bindings change what a scan reports, so they get listed here like\n any other user-visible change. A finding that suddenly appears in your CI is a\n behaviour change, even if no code moved.\n \n+## [Unreleased]\n+","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"c8ea773c-1241-4a1e-94e1-b32ea2545cb2","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:14:24.580Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"commit 31d5f496e3978c99ed91893c6cc388735759fe90\nAuthor: vyql bot <vyql.bot@vyprsec.ai>\nDate:   Wed Sep 16 23:10:22 2026 +0000\n\n    engine: hold a one-graph bounded scan's graph in RAM when the ceiling fits it\n    \n    -max-ram armed the disk-backed BadgerGraph store for every scan it\n    bounded. For a target whose graph fits in the ceiling, the store costs\n    more than the graph it spares: its detail buffer, write path and caches\n    sit resident alongside a structural core that never leaves RAM in\n    either store, so a multi-package repository that lowers to a\n    million-odd nodes peaked several times its in-RAM footprint (measured\n    4626MiB through the disk store against 1220MiB in RAM on the same\n    repository) and the memory safety stop ended the scan before the first\n    rule ran — an empty report from a 4GB ceiling the scan easily afforded.\n    \n    The partition planner's own affordability measure now decides the\n    store: a target PlanPartitions leaves unpartitioned is a target one\n    graph holds, and that graph is built on the int-indexed in-RAM store —\n    the same store a partitioned scan already builds per partition. A\n    graph the ceiling cannot hold still spills: a target over the\n    one-graph limit is partitioned, and a run that must serialise one\n    graph (-stats, graph-json with review flags) keeps the disk-backed\n    bound. Unbounded scans are untouched; a scan whose cache directories\n    are unwritable keeps its existing in-RAM fallback.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\ndiff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md\nindex cd1faed11f..f3d7c922d1 100644\n--- a/CHANGELOG.md\n+++ b/CHANGELOG.md\n@@ -8,6 +8,29 @@ New rules and bindings change what a scan reports, so they get listed here like\n any other user-visible change. A finding that suddenly appears in your CI is a\n behaviour change, even if no code moved.\n \n+## [Unreleased]\n+\n+### Fixed\n+\n===\ndiff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md\nindex cd1faed11f..f3d7c922d1 100644\n--- a/CHANGELOG.md\n+++ b/CHANGELOG.md\n@@ -8,6 +8,29 @@ New rules and bindings change what a scan reports, so they get listed here like\n any other user-visible change. A finding that suddenly appears in your CI is a\n behaviour change, even if no code moved.\n \n+## [Unreleased]\n+","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
