 tree (git status showed \"deleted: telemetry.json\"). And harness files modified. `git rm --cached` works regardless.\n\nActually — hmm, one consideration: are these files perhaps .gitignore'd in main and force-added by the sweep? Let me check .gitignore. If .gitignore lists them, then after `git rm --cached` they'd be ignored → the sweep can't re-add them easily (unless it uses -f). That would be ideal. Let me check.\n\n2. Merge origin/main: `git merge origin/main` → conflict in CHANGELOG.md → resolve with `git checkout origin/main -- CHANGELOG.md` → commit merge.\n\n3. Build + test suite.\n\n4. Recreate dense corpora; measure base (origin/main binary) vs fix (branch binary):\n   - /tmp/dense3 (3 files x 2000 functions)\n   - /tmp/densepy (12 files x 2000 functions)\n   Expected: base rc=1 at ~3.3-3.4GB with empty report; fix rc=0 ~2.6-2.7GB with report.\n\n5. openrazer: base vs fix, findings parity.\n\n6. Bench corpora parity (BenchmarkJava, BenchmarkPython, ports).\n\n7. RealVuln full corpus parity if time allows (66 repos — the previous session did it; it's the strongest evidence; runs take a while but are just scans; let me try in background).\n\n8. Failing-test check at new base: apply only the test files to a pristine origin/main worktree, run → must fail. Then with fix → pass. I can do this in a temp worktree (git worktree add /tmp/basetree origin/main — wait, worktrees share the repo; creating a worktree in /tmp is fine and doesn't touch other branches. Or simpler: clone to /tmp. The repo is local; `git worktree add --detach /tmp/base-main origin/main` is cheap. But note the campaign instructions warn the stash is shared — worktrees are fine. I'll clean it up after.)\n\n9. Restore harness files to committed state (after my rm --cached, \"committed state\" = absent from index; the files remain on disk untracked — that's the harness's business). Actually after rm --cached + commit, HEAD has no harness files; the working tree files are untracked dirt. \"Clean