{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260919132147284c230e84224b26","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed:\n- 3×500 (1500 funcs, one graph): peak 2603 MB — that's ONE graph in RAM.\n- 3×1000: partitioned into 3 partitions (one per file), peak 2670 MB (per-partition in-RAM store + overhead), and it took 74.7s (3 partitions × ~25s).\n\nSo the fix's one-graph cost: 750 funcs → 885MB; 1500 funcs → 2603MB. Ratio: 2× functions → 2.94× memory. That's super-linear — consistent with a quadratic term (4·F·L with L ∝ F → quadratic in F). 750→885MB, 1500→2603MB: if purely quadratic, 4× → 3.5GB expected; measured 2603MB — mixed linear+quadratic. And 3×2000 (6000 funcs, 3.8MB source) got partitioned (like 3×1000 — wait, why did 3×2000 die then? It was partitioned into 3 partitions and STILL died at 3.3GiB. Because each partition = one 1.27MB file with 2000 functions → per-partition graph ≈ 2000-func file → the same quadratic term within the partition → each partition needs >3.3GB. Compare: 3×500 file = 320KB, 500 funcs → one graph of 1500 funcs = 2603MB total including 2×... hmm per-file: 500-func file ≈ 867MB. A 2000-func file: quadratic → 16× the 500-func file's per-file cost ≈ way over 3.3GB. Yes — that's why the fix dies on dense3: each 1.27MB/2000-func file alone crosses the stop inside its partition.)\n\nAnd the base on 3×500 would presumably be even worse (plus 512-token re-carriage per function — though at 500 functions the module tokens cap at 512 quickly, so base ≈ fix + 500×512×2 nodes ≈ +512k nodes... significant too).\n\nSo the remaining quadratic term on the CURRENT fix is real and it's the moduleContext (joined string literals of the module) riding on 4 calls per function — F functions → moduleContext ∝ F → 4F·L ∝ F². Also bodyText rides 4× per function but bodyText is per-function (constant per function) → linear overall. The module-literal context is THE quadratic term.\n\nLet me verify the hypothesis directly: instrument via a quick experiment — generate a corpus with the SAME shape but withOUT string literals in functions (docstrings/keys/messages replaced by integers?). Then moduleContext ≈ tiny, and if the peak drops dramatically → confirmed.\n\nMy template's literals per function: docstring (`\"\"\"Handler {i}: ...\"\"\"` ~45B), \"field_{i}\" ×2-3, \"raw_{i}\", \"size_{i}\", \"handler_{i}: oversized field_{i}\", \"error\": \"missing field_{i}\", \"oversized\". ≈ 150 bytes per function. At F=2000: L ≈ 300KB per file. 4·F·L = 4×2000×300KB = 2.4GB per file. Boom — matches dying in-partition at 3.3GB.\n\nBut WAIT — before designing a fix, I must ask: is the fix's job to make MY corpus pass, or the previous session's? The gap says dense multi-package Python must complete under 4GB/180s. A real repository (the blocked rank: openrazer — its Python files have hundreds of functions with many literals) — openrazer at fix: 941MB, fine. The gap's \"multi-package code\" — the corpus that reproduces. The mandated repro is whatever I demonstrate; the honest bar: a dense multi-package corpus whose per-file literal mass × function count is REALISTIC. My template is realistic-ish (every function has a docstring and messages). The previous session's lighter corpus passed with 2.6GB on 6000 functions ACROSS 3 files (2000/file) — wait no, theirs died at base and passed at fix with 6000 functions over 3 files = 2000/file — MY identical shape (2000/file) DIES at fix. So their template must have been much lighter per function (few literals, small bodies).\n\nHmm. So the previous fix genuinely closed their repro but the margin was their corpus's thinness. Mine exposes the same disease one layer deeper: moduleContext re-carriage. \n\nNow — decision. The role: \"The smallest change that makes the behaviour expressible.\" The behaviour: whole-repo scan under 4GB/180s completes with a report for dense multi-package Python. The branch already carries two economy fixes. Do I add a third (moduleContext), or do I accept the branch as-is and demonstrate on a corpus matching the previous session's?\n\nDanger of adding more: each economy change risks changing observable facts (what bindings can match). The moduleContext join approach: the value is ALREADY one joined string — the cost is that the SAME big string is duplicated into 4·F call args / Const nodes / str_args. If the graph STORE dedupes equal Const payloads, the observable behaviour (values on calls) doesn't change AT ALL — only memory. That would be a clean engine-layer fix: intern Const payload strings in the store/lowering.\n\nWhere does the duplication physically happen? Need to find out:\n1. nir.Const{Value: c.moduleContext} — Go string headers share backing → NIR is cheap.\n2. Lowering mints a Const NODE per Const arg. Node stores Value string — if it stores the same header, cheap.\n3. BUT: str_args on the call node — if lowering builds str_args by joining/concatenating arg values into a NEW string per call, that's a fresh L-byte allocation per call → 5·F·L bytes of NEW strings — THE blow-up. Let me check how str_args is built in lowering.go.\n\nLet me look at evalCall's Const handling and str_args construction.","signature":"471fb8b145fc4aea9e40ba60"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"d873c74f-e5aa-4fa9-9f72-e418466a537d","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:22:12.639Z"}
