{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260919132237d2cd36b9bc0e49c6","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key mechanics in evalCall:\n- For each Const arg: `l.eval(a, sc)` mints a Const NODE (with the Value string, presumably as a prop), an Arg node, flow edges.\n- `collectValTokens(a, \"\", &valToks)` collects the literal's value into valToks.\n- `strArgs = strings.Join(valToks, \"\\x00\")` — a FRESH joined string per call, containing all literal values concatenated.\n\nSo for each function's `analysis.function.context.end` call: valToks = [lang=python, name=..., bodyText, compact(bodyText), moduleContext, ...tokens..., joined module tokens]. strArgs = join of ALL of those ≈ 2×bodyText + moduleContext + ... ≈ per function a fresh string of size ~2·bodyText + L. Over F functions: F·(2·bodyText + L) where L ∝ F → quadratic. PLUS the Const nodes: does the Const node store the value as a prop string? l.eval of nir.Const → mints node with props value=... — the prop value shares the backing array with c.moduleContext (same Go string) IF the store keeps the string as-is. IntStore AddNode — does it copy props into an arena? Let me check IntStore.AddNode.\n\nActually — the dominant physical copies:\n1. str_args fresh join per call: 5 context calls per function each get a strArgs join containing moduleContext (for 4 of them) + bodyText (4 of them) etc. → per function ≈ 4·(L + ~2·bodyText) + localend(2·localBodyText) ≈ 4L + ... → 4FL bytes total. At F=2000, L=300KB: 2.4GB. THE blow-up.\n2. Const node props: value=moduleContext shared backing → cheap IF IntStore doesn't copy. Let me check.\n\nSo the honest engine-layer fix: the str_args join is per-call fresh; but its CONTENT is what value-matching reads. Can't avoid the join semantically... unless — the join of valToks for the analysis.function.context.* calls includes bodyText and moduleContext — do value matchers actually match against these huge strings? They do substring/`contains` matching presumably (`val`/`nval` on sinks). A binding could ask: function context contains \"secret_key\" — matching over bodyText is exactly the feature. So the strings must be there.\n\nOptions:\nA. Cap the sizes: bodyText is per-function (linear, fine). moduleContext (L ∝ F) is the quadratic one. Cap moduleContext length (e.g. first N KB)? Loses facts for big modules — same objection as token cap lowering: \"the cap is observable behaviour\". But NOTE: moduleTokens is already capped at 512 — the structured token walk. The LITERAL context (moduleContext) has NO cap. Hmm — is that an oversight or a feature? pyModuleLiteralContext joins ALL string literals in the module. A cap analogous to pyContextBaseTokenLimit (512 tokens) would be consistent with the existing design: the module's literal context capped at 512 literals (or bytes). But it changes facts: a rule matching a literal that appears after the cap point would stop firing on files with >512 literals. Is there any binding matching over module literal context? This is risk.\n\nB. Intern/dedupe the str_args payload at the STORE layer: IntStore could intern equal strings (a map[string]string keeping one canonical). But the str_args strings DIFFER per call (name=X, bodyText differ) — no dedupe possible. The COMMON part is moduleContext but each call's str_args is a distinct concatenation. No.\n\nC. Change the per-function re-carriage: moduleContext rides on 4 of 5 calls. Could the source/context/sink/end calls NOT each carry the full moduleContext? Which readers read which call? If value matching on `analysis.function.context` (the `.context` call) reads module literals, maybe only ONE of the four needs it... but that changes facts per call path — bindings may target `.source` or `.sink` or `.end` specifically.\n\nD. Make moduleContext itself bounded by DESIGN like the tokens: cap the number of literal tokens joined (e.g., 512 literals), mirroring pyContextBaseTokenLimit. The moduleContext string then has bounded size... no wait — 512 literals × unbounded literal length (a triple-quoted docstring can be KBs) → still up to MBs. Cap by BYTES then (e.g. 64KB). Hmm.\n\nE. The genuinely engine-layer fix: str_args as a JOIN of fresh strings is inherent, but the STORE's per-node cost for these long strings — the strings must exist once per call. 4·F·L is inherent to re-carriage design... The only structural fix is not re-carrying L per function.\n\nWait — wait. Let me step back. Is re-carriage per function of the module's literal context even SENSIBLE design? It's the same fact (the module's literal context) duplicated on every function's context calls — identical VALUE on all of them (the Const value is byte-identical across functions; only str_args differs because it concatenates name/bodyText). The Const nodes: value byte-identical, loc differs (function's loc). 4·F Const nodes each carrying prop value=moduleContext — IF the store dedupes prop PAYLOADS by (value) — Go strings share backing, so prop value strings cost 16 bytes header each. Actually then Const nodes are CHEAP.\n\nSo the real cost is str_args: 4 fresh F-sized joins per function. Hmm, AND the presence-matcher path reads Consts. AND value matching reads str_args.\n\nHmm, hold on — let me re-measure what's actually resident. Maybe I'm wrong about which term dominates. Better: instrument directly. Let me write a small Go test/program that lowers one 2000-function file and reports the graph's memory + the str_args total bytes. Actually quicker: patch nothing, use pprof? Overkill.\n\nCheap experiment: my template WITHOUT string literals (replace literals with int constants) at 3×2000. If peak collapses → moduleContext confirmed. Keep everything else identical. Let me do that first — it's one run and decisive.\n\nAlso do the BASE at 3×500 to compare base vs fix on the same one-graph corpus.\n\nThen decide the fix. Let me also reconsider: is fixing THIS in my lane and scope? The task gap: memory stop kills whole-repo scan on multi-package Python. The branch's mission is exactly this. The previous fix closed one term; if the repro (a realistic dense corpus) still dies, the gap is NOT closed — the campaign would measure with their own corpora; if their corpus is like mine, the PR fails measurement (\"heaviest corpus must finish inside the memory ceiling\"). Actually WAIT — important: the campaign's merge review measures \"every benchmark corpus ... and the heaviest corpus finishes inside the memory ceiling\". The previous session's PR was never reviewed/merged. If the campaign's benchmark corpora include dense Python, and their heaviest corpus died at base and passed at fix with their margins... unknown.\n\nMy duty: the smallest change that makes the behaviour expressible + failing test. The behaviour is the scan completing under the mandated caps. If I demonstrate base-dies/fix-completes on a corpus, and the campaign's own measurement accepts it, done. The risk is leaving a known quadratic term that their heaviest corpus trips. Given the previous session's extensive parity work, ADDING more surgery risks detection changes; NOT adding it risks the same \"empty report\" on heavier corpora.\n\nLet me first get the data (no-literals experiment + base@500), then decide.","signature":"5f00db7877614a87b886131d"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"5cb960d0-8fb6-41fa-a10c-ad02c09e0dde","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:23:10.813Z"}
