{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260919132434136e7d38597b40af","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Very interesting:\n\n1. **base on /tmp/nolit** (no literals, 6000 funcs, partitioned): rc=0, peak 2298 MB — base COMPLETES. So on nolit, base passes too (the 512-token re-carriage alone at 2000 funcs/file = 2000×512×2 = 2M nodes/file... apparently fits: 2.3GB peak). So nolit doesn't distinguish base from fix — NOT a valid repro for the gap. The previous session's corpus must have had literals too — and their fix run peaked at 2.6GB — consistent with a LIGHTER literal load: with L smaller, base (with token term + literal term) died, fix (literal term only) squeaked under at 2.6GB.\n\n2. **base on scale500** (1500 funcs WITH literals, one graph): rc=1, died at 3.43GB. **fix on scale500**: rc=0 at 2603MB. So scale500 IS a valid base-dies/fix-lives repro on the current tree. Good — the branch as-is does close the gap on scale500.\n\n3. **fix on dense3/densepy (2000+ funcs/file with my literals)**: still dies → the literal term remains open.\n\nSo my options crystallize:\n\nOption 1: Ship the branch as-is; demonstrate on scale500 (base dies at 3.4GB, fix completes 2.6GB). The gap's wording: \"when the Python lowering and graph construction over multi-package code exceeds it\" — with a corpus where the fix survives. But I now KNOW a realistic heavier corpus still writes the empty report — the same gap, one layer deeper. The campaign's merge review measures every benchmark corpus; if its heaviest Python corpus is shaped like mine, the PR is refused again (that may be EXACTLY why the previous attempt wasn't merged: their benchmark measurement may have found the heaviest corpus still failing... though no review is recorded).\n\nHmm wait, actually — was the previous PR refused by measurement? No reviews/comments on PR #242, reviewDecision empty. It just sat. The campaign's STATUS showed rank 2794 as \"engine-gap\" with PR #396 — hmm, #396 ≠ #242. Interesting — the STATUS.md \"PR\" column says #396 for rank 2794 but GitHub PR for this branch is #242. The #396 in STATUS.md is likely a different numbering (their own DB's PR id?) — other engine rows also show #360, #361... those are high numbers not matching GitHub PR numbers (the branch PRs on GitHub are #2xx). So STATUS \"PR\" column is the campaign's internal request id, not GitHub. Whatever.\n\nWhy did the campaign never process PR #242? Possibly its mergeability check failed (after f24ca80320 landed Sep 18 and conflicted CHANGELOG.md, GitHub reports mergeable:false — maybe the campaign skips unmergeable PRs). My merge fixes that. \n\nOption 2: Extend the branch with a third change closing the literal term, with a failing test, keeping output parity. Then demonstrate on my dense corpora: base dies AND fix completes even at 2000×3.\n\nWhat would the third change be, at the right layer, smallest, parity-preserving?\n\nThe moduleContext string is joined once per module (`c.moduleContext`) and re-carried as a Const on 4 calls per function. The VALUE is identical across all functions of the module. What actually costs memory:\n(a) 4·F Const nodes carrying prop value=<L bytes> — Go strings share backing → each node costs ~a map entry + 16B header IF IntStore stores props as map[string]string with the same string. Need to verify IntStore.AddNode doesn't copy/serialize.\n(b) 4·F str_args joins — fresh F-sized strings per call: 4·F·L bytes of NEW allocations. This is unavoidable per-call if str_args must contain the module context... \n\nWait — does str_args NEED to contain the huge literals? What reads str_args? Value matching (`val`/`nval` predicates on sinks; node value matching \"reads the call's str_args\"). The presence predicates walk Consts (that was the previous fix's analysis). For value matching: a binding puts val=foo on a sink path analysis.function.context — matching = does str_args contain \"foo\"? So str_args must carry the module literals for value matching to see them. If I cap moduleContext, val matching over module literals changes for big modules.\n\nHmm hold on, hold on. Let me reconsider the actual semantics. Where do the four calls differ?\n- source/context/sink/end all carry `args` (which includes moduleContext).\n- Value matching on str_args of these analysis calls.\n\nBut here's the thing: the campaign merge review measures score movements on benchmark corpora. The previous fix kept output parity by joining tokens with \\x00 (readers split). For moduleContext, any change to its content risks parity.\n\nWhat about the ENGINE-layer fix that changes NO bytes anywhere: make the IntStore/lowering NOT duplicate the payload. The four calls per function each build `strArgs = strings.Join(valToks, \"\\x00\")`. valToks = [lang=python, name=..., bodyText, compact, moduleContext, ...]. The join creates a fresh string. F functions → 4F joins each ~L+2B bytes. The DATA is inherently 4F·(L+2B) — unless the str_args strings share structure, which Go strings can't.\n\nSo the only structural reductions:\n(i) Don't put moduleContext into str_args (keep it as a Const arg + Const node prop, shared backing, cheap). Value matching via str_args would no longer see module literals — PARITY RISK unless value matching also reads Const arg node values. Hmm — \"value matching reads the call's str_args, which is the same joined string\" (previous session's words for the token join: they JOINED the tokens into the str_args so value matching still sees them). So value matching ONLY reads str_args. Removing moduleContext from str_args breaks value matching over module literals. NO.\n\n(ii) Cap the module literal context — mirrors pyContextBaseTokenLimit for structured tokens. The module literal context today: ALL literals, unbounded count AND length. A cap changes facts only for modules over the cap. How many real modules exceed any sane cap? openrazer: py files are modest. The bench corpora: BenchmarkPython — fine. RealVuln: fine. Parity risk concentrated on huge generated Python files — rare in real repos. Hmm, but \"rare\" is not \"never\" and it's exactly the dense-corpus shape the gap is about... capping would make MY dense corpus pass (moduleContext bounded → L constant → linear overall). Wait no — cap at C bytes: per-function str_args ≈ 2·bodyText + C. F functions → F·(2·bodyText + 4C) → LINEAR. My dense3 (2000/file): bodyText ~600B ×2 + 4×C. With C=64KB: 2000×(1.2KB+256KB)=513MB/file — hmm 4 calls × 64KB = 256KB per function — still heavy but linear. With C=16KB: 2000×65KB=130MB/file. Fine.\n\nBut is a byte cap the right shape? The TOKENS are capped at 512 count. For literals, the analogue: cap the number of literal TOKENS collected (e.g. 512 literals). Length per literal can still be huge (a 1MB docstring) → cap bytes too? pyContextCompactUnbounded(text) already rides per function (bounded by body size). Hmm.\n\n(iii) Zip/join like before doesn't apply (already one string).\n\n(iv) Reduce the NUMBER of calls carrying it: source/context/sink/end — 4 copies. Do all four need the literal context? Bindings target these paths by name (analysis.function.context.source / .context / .sink / .end). Any could be the anchor a binding picks. Dropping from any changes that path's value-matching surface. PARITY RISK.\n\n(v) Store-layer: intern Prop VALUES by content across nodes — doesn't help str_args (distinct strings).\n\n(vi) str_args structural sharing: no.\n\nSo the realistic in-lane, parity-preserving options are (ii) a bounded module literal context [changes facts only over the bound] — hmm, wait, actually, is there something smarter?\n\nWhat about making moduleContext not ride on the per-function calls at all, but instead have the readers fall back to the module-level analysis.module.context call (which already carries the module's tokens AND text AND compact AND literals? Let me check pyModuleContext: args = [lang=python, text(full module text!), compact, moduleTokens...]. Note: pyModuleContext carries the FULL MODULE TEXT as a Const (line 906) — unbounded too! For a 1.27MB file that's 1.27MB once per file — linear, fine.\n\nInteresting: the module context call already carries the full module text. So moduleContext (joined literals) on the function calls is a SUBSET of what analysis.module.context already carries (as `text`). The per-function re-carriage exists so that FUNCTION-scoped presence predicates can see module-level literals within the function's lexical scope (presence scopes are per function — the previous session established that: \"presence scopes are per function\"). And value matching on the function's context calls.\n\nOK here's my decision framework. The mandate: smallest change; failing test; parity measured. Two candidate paths:\n\nPATH A (ship as-is): repro = scale500-style corpus (base dies, fix lives). Known residual: denser literal-heavy corpora still die. The gap's own words — \"Python lowering and graph construction over multi-package code exceeds it\" — my dense3 at fix still exhibits the empty-report behaviour. I'd be writing a verdict that says the gap is closed while holding a repro that says it isn't. The previous session did exactly that (their corpus passed, mine doesn't). The campaign re-issued the task... though probably for the conflict/hygiene reasons.\n\nPATH B (extend with the literal bound): add a cap on the module literal context — BUT this changes observable facts for modules over the cap (a val=... binding over a literal beyond the cap stops matching). Parity on the measured corpora likely holds (cap high enough that no real corpus crosses it, low enough that dense corpora fit). E.g. cap at 512 literal tokens (mirroring pyContextBaseTokenLimit) — a module with more than 512 literals keeps the first 512. My dense file: 2000 functions × ~7 literals = 14000 literals → capped at 512 → moduleContext ≈ 512×~25B ≈ 13KB → per function 4×13KB=52KB → 2000 funcs = 104MB/file. Passes easily.\n\nParity risk: does any benchmark corpus have a Python module with >512 string literals where a finding depends on a literal beyond position 512? Possible but unlikely; and I can MEASURE parity on all the corpora (openrazer, bench, RealVuln 66 repos, 10160 spec subtests) — the previous session built that harness; I can rerun it.\n\nHmm wait, but hold on. Is capping the literal context really \"the behaviour expressible\" fix? Let me reconsider the design intent: moduleTokens capped at 512 (the structured walk). The literal walk uncapped looks like an oversight rather than intent — the structured token walk has BOTH a count cap (pyContextBaseTokenLimit=512) applied via `add()`. The literal walk (`pyModuleLiteralContext`) has no cap. Capping it at the SAME 512-token limit is symmetric, in-file, small.\n\nLet me check pyContextBaseTokenLimit's value and whether there's a byte cap anywhere. And check what pyContextCompactUnbounded does (unbounded = deliberately NOT compacted?). Names suggest: compact functions bound size; \"Unbounded\" variant doesn't. The module context call carries pyContextCompactUnbounded(text) — full text uncompacted. So the design accepts big strings ONCE per module but the per-function re-carriage multiplies them — the design flaw is the multiplication, and the previous fix addressed the tokens term of exactly that.\n\nSo the cleanest SYMMETRIC fix: cap the module literal context re-carriage at the same 512-literal count. Actually wait — should the cap be on token COUNT (literals) only? A module with 100 literals each 10KB (docstrings) = 1MB moduleContext → still 4·F·1MB. Ugh. For robustness: cap count at 512 AND total bytes? Introducing a new byte constant is more design surface... but the failing case (giant literals) is real (triple-quoted license headers!). Hmm, but pyModuleLiteralContext collects ALL string CONTENT — including docstrings of every function. A 2000-function module with one-line docstrings: 2000 literals → count cap handles it. A module with 10 giant docstrings: count cap doesn't; byte cap would.\n\nLet me look at how the file's own text is handled per function: bodyText rides as `nir.Const{Value: bodyText}` per function — 4 calls × bodyText per function → 4·F·B bytes fresh via str_args join too! bodyText is inherently per-function; 4× duplication of the body text in str_args (source, context, sink, end all join it). Plus compact(bodyText) — a COMPACTED copy. So per function the str_args carry bodyText ~3× + compact 1× + localBodyText 2× (local.end: localBodyText + Fields-joined) ≈ 6·B per function → linear but with constant 6. Fine — linear terms are the scan's honest cost.\n\nOK so let me now decide between shipping as-is vs extending. Actually — wait. Let me first check ONE more thing: what does the CAMPAIGN's own heaviest corpus look like? The merge review measures benchmark corpora at /campaign/bench (Real-Vuln-Benchmark, plus /tmp/bench has BenchmarkJava/BenchmarkPython/ports). Let me check the densest Python file in BenchmarkPython and RealVuln — if the largest literal-count module there is < 512 literals, the cap is provably parity-neutral on everything the campaign measures.","signature":"1f5d6b86804442ac8ee5c004"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"9358cf5d-fb10-4ade-b180-b1109bd33771","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:25:31.385Z"}
