{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091913255882f0cd5406c1462a","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the previous test's corpus shape: `moduleTokenSource` — one-liner functions with 2 literals each ('field_%d' and '<b>...'). At 400 functions: 800 literals → moduleContext ≈ 800×12B ≈ 10KB. Per function 4×10KB=40KB → 400 funcs = 16MB. Fine. But at 2000 functions/file (their dense3!): moduleContext = 4000 literals ≈ 50KB; per function 4×(50KB + 2·60B) ≈ 200KB; ×2000 = 400MB/file ×3 files = 1.2GB + the rest — consistent with their 2.6GB peak. So their corpus DID trip the literal term too — they just stayed under the wire (their functions were tiny). My corpus (heavier bodies + more literals per function) goes over.\n\nSo the literal term was ALWAYS the next margin. The right thing: close it properly, in this same branch, with a failing test and measured parity. The campaign re-issued the task — this is the rework.\n\nNow, the fix shape. Let me think really carefully about semantics.\n\n`pyModuleLiteralContext(root)`: joins ALL string-literal TEXTS in the module with \"\\n\". This is \"the module's literal context\". It rides on every function's context args (4 calls). Its purposes (what can match it):\n1. Value matching (val/contains) on str_args of the function's context/source/sink/end calls: a binding asks \"does this function's context mention 'password='\" etc.\n2. Presence predicates' Const walk: Const nodes whose value is the literal context, scoped to the function — contains/containsAny over the Const value.\n\nKey insight about the SHAPE of the fix: the moduleContext value is IDENTICAL for all functions in a module. The per-function args each embed the same L-byte string. The graph cost = 4F·L via str_args + F Const nodes (shared backing — need to confirm IntStore doesn't copy).\n\nLet me confirm IntStore.AddNode's handling of Props: does it copy strings? Go map[string]string assignment copies the HEADER only. The props map per node: `value` → shared string. So Const nodes are cheap — 4 map entries per function... wait, no: `l.eval(a, sc)` for nir.Const — how does it mint the Const node? Let me check. It probably does l.node(\"Const\", props{\"value\": v}). The props map holds the shared string header. Cheap.\n\nSo the resident blow-up = str_args fresh joins. 4 per function, each ≈ L + 2·B + C(compact) + tokens. Sum over F functions ≈ 4FL.\n\nWait — is that really it? Let me sanity check with numbers: my dense file: F=2000, literals ≈ 2000×7=14000, L ≈ 14000×25B ≈ 350KB. 4FL = 4×2000×350KB = 2.8GB per file. Yes — matches dying in-partition.\n\nNow — fix candidates for the str_args term specifically:\n\nThe str_args of each of the 4 calls = Join([lang, name, bodyText, compact(bodyText), moduleContext, classTokens..., decorators..., fnTokens..., delegated..., moduleTokens-joined]). Note this whole join is dominated by bodyText (2·B appears twice: bodyText + compact) + moduleContext.\n\nIDEA: What if str_args for these calls joined with the module context ONCE — i.e., the join is per-call by design... no way around per-call joins without changing value-matching semantics.\n\nIDEA 2: Reduce the number of calls that carry moduleContext from 4 to 1 (the `.end` call, same as the tokens re-carriage which is ALSO end-only per the previous fix — see test: \"the re-carriage is the end call's alone\"!). WAIT. Look at the test: module_ TOKENS ride ONLY on the end call (otherCallsWithModuleLiteral must be 0). So the previous fix moved the token re-carriage to end-only. But moduleContext (literals) still rides on ALL FOUR (args is shared by source/context/sink/end).\n\nInteresting asymmetry! Was the tokens-on-end-only shape PRE-EXISTING (base also had tokens on end only)? Yes — the base code appended moduleTokens to `endArgs` only (that's what the fix replaced with one joined literal). So the module TOKENS were always end-only, but the module LITERAL context rides on all four calls via `args`.\n\nSo which call do bindings read for module literal facts? If a binding anchors on `analysis.function.context` (the .context call) with val=..., it reads .context's str_args which includes moduleContext. Changing which calls carry it = changing those surfaces. Can't drop without parity risk — must MEASURE.\n\nHmm, OK here's another thought — the SIZE of the join per call. str_args = join(valToks). valToks includes bodyText AND compact(bodyText) AND moduleContext AND moduleTokens-joined AND function tokens. For value matching, `contains` semantics. What if instead of joining the FULL moduleContext into str_args, we joined a COMPACTED moduleContext (pyContextCompact — bounded)? Compact = strip whitespace etc.? pyContextCompactUnbounded(text) is used for the compact copy. There's pyContextCompact (bounded?) — let me look at these functions. If compacting bounds the size (e.g., caps at N bytes), then str_args would carry the bounded compact... but the Const still carries full. Value matching reads str_args — would see compact version — PARITY RISK for val matching (a val spanning removed whitespace breaks).\n\nOK let me stop guessing at shapes and get the profiling data — measure exactly where the bytes are in the store for a 2000-function file: (a) sum of str_args lengths, (b) sum of Const prop value lengths, (c) node count. Then I'll know precisely what each candidate saves. Also measure how much of str_args total is moduleContext vs bodyText.","signature":"9adff835d4f34e4384c64c8d"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"9346c5ec-ca32-4060-ad30-2734e115c7d3","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:26:22.876Z"}
