{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091913273087fff8081b0e46c1","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Found it. The call node's props include:\n- `argN` props: the Arg node ids (small strings — ids).\n- `litN` props (usg.LitPropKey(i)): the FIRST LITERAL TOKEN of arg i — for the context calls, arg 4 = moduleContext (index 4: [0]=lang [1]=name [2]=bodyText [3]=compact [4]=moduleContext...). So lit4 = moduleContext — the full L bytes copied into the props map... wait, `first` is `litFirst` = the constStrVal → for the moduleContext Const arg, litFirst = moduleContext (L bytes, SHARED backing — the prop value is the same string!). Hmm — but len() counts it per occurrence in my profile: 2000 functions × 4 calls... no wait, props are on the CALL node (one per call — the call node has litN for its args). 5 context calls per function... each of the 4 calls carrying moduleContext as arg[4] gets lit4=moduleContext. So the profile's 1153 MiB lit4 = 4·F·L LOGICAL bytes — but is it RESIDENT? The string header points to the shared c.moduleContext backing — resident cost is L once! UNLESS something copies. props map[k]v stores the header. So lit4 = 1153 MiB LOGICAL but only ~350KB resident (one file's L).\n\nHmm!! So my profile's byte totals overcount — they count shared strings repeatedly. The REAL resident question needs allocation-level accounting. The scan dies at 3.3GiB though — SOMETHING allocates gigabytes fresh. The candidates for FRESH allocations:\n1. str_args joins: 4 per function, each a fresh string of ~(L + 2B) — F·4·(L+2B) ≈ 2000×4×(350KB+1.2KB) ≈ 2.8GB. FRESH. This alone explains the death.\n2. c.text(n) — substrings of source: `c.src[start:end]` in Go — SUBSTRING SHARES backing with src. So bodyText shares the file's source buffer! Not fresh.\n3. pyContextCompactUnbounded(bodyText) — builds a NEW string (strips whitespace) → fresh per function, bounded by body size — linear·2B. OK.\n4. strings.Join for module context tokens (the previous fix's join) — computed ONCE per module? c.moduleTokens joined per FUNCTION! Look: `\"module_\" + strings.Join(c.moduleTokens, \"\\x00module_\")` — INSIDE pyFunctionContext — a FRESH join PER FUNCTION! L_tok ≈ 512×25B ≈ 13KB × F = 26MB. Linear, ok-ish. Wait no — F×13KB = 2000×13KB = 26MB per file. Fine.\n5. strings.Join(strings.Fields(localBodyText), \"\") — fresh per function, ~B.\n6. The NIR itself: nir.Program holds all modules' stmts — the 5 calls' args slices etc. — GC'd after lowering? The program is dropped after Lower? In a scan the NIR for all files may be held until lowering completes... The scanner lowers per module? If the whole NIR of all files is built first, then lowered — NIR holds shared substrings — fine.\n\nSo the resident blow-up = the str_args joins: ~2.8GB fresh per dense file. CONFIRMED as the dominant term. (The 3.17GiB str_args logical ≈ mostly fresh joins: sum over call nodes of join length. Const nodes' StrArgs are shared. 563k nodes... 5.8KiB/node avg — the call nodes' joins dominate.)\n\nSo ANY fix must reduce the per-call str_args join content for these context calls. Options re-examined:\n\n(i) Cap moduleContext content (fewer/shorter literals) → L shrinks → joins shrink. Changes facts (drops literals) for files over the cap. openrazer mouse.py (1910 literals) — facts change there. Risk: parity on openrazer/RealVuln measurable — I can measure.\n\n(ii) Remove moduleContext from `args` entirely (no re-carriage) → joins lose L. Bindings val-matching module literals on function context calls lose that surface — parity risk, previous session rejected the analogous move for tokens.\n\n(iii) Keep moduleContext on ONE call (say .end, mirroring the tokens re-carriage!) instead of 4 → joins lose L on 3 of 4 calls: F·L instead of 4F·L — 4× saving. Bindings anchored on source/.context/.sink lose the module-literal surface; .end keeps it. Parity measurable. Hmm — is there an existing reason tokens ride ONLY on end? Look at base: moduleTokens were end-only from the start. Maybe deliberately: the END call is the \"module facts at function end\" carrier. So moving moduleContext to end-only is SYMMETRIC with the existing design! Let me check git history for why moduleTokens were end-only... Actually the test says \"the re-carriage is the end call's alone\" — treated as the designed shape.\n\n(iv) Keep it on all 4 calls but make the JOIN cheap — impossible; join must contain it.\n\nSo (iii) is the design-symmetric fix: module literal context joins the module tokens on the end call only. It preserves EVERY literal (no cap, no drops) and every reader that reads the end call. Readers anchored on .context/.source/.sink lose module-literal visibility... Do any bindings actually read module literals off the non-end calls? MEASURABLE: full spec suite (10160 subtests) + openrazer + bench + RealVuln parity.\n\nWait — before (iii), let me reconsider (v): does moduleContext need to be in the per-function `args` as a Const at all — the Const NODES are cheap (shared backing), the cost is only the str_args join which contains every Const arg's value. The join is built from valToks = collectValTokens over ALL Const args. Hmm what if collectValTokens SKIPPED the huge module-context Const for the non-end calls... that's exactly (iii) implemented at the lowering layer vs the frontend layer. LAYER: the frontend shapes the args (python.go) — right layer = frontend (same as previous fix: \"The economy had to be bought where the facts are shaped, in the frontend\").\n\nSo (iii) = python.go: move `nir.Const{Value: c.moduleContext}` out of the shared `args` and append it only to `endArgs` (like the module tokens). Let me ALSO reconsider: bodyText rides in args (shared by 4 calls): bodyText is a SUBSTRING of c.src — shared backing; each call's join copies it → 4·F·2B... B ≈ 600B → 4×2000×1.2KB ≈ 9.6MB/file. Fine, linear, leave alone.\n\nAdditional consideration — the `sink` call: sinkArgs = [tmp(Name)] + args. The Name eval — fine.\n\nNow, what about `analysis.module.context` (module-level, once per file): carries `text` = FULL module source (~1.1MB for dense file) + compact + 512 tokens. Once per file — linear. Fine.\n\nLet me now quantify (iii)'s saving on my profile shape: joins per function after (iii): 3 calls × (2B + C + fnTokens) + 1 end call × (2B + C + fnTokens + L + L_tok). Total per function ≈ 4×(2B+C) + L + L_tok ≈ 4×2.4KB + 350KB + 13KB ≈ 373KB → F=2000 → 726MB/file?? STILL BIG. Hmm. L=350KB per file is just large. 3 files → 2.2GB. STILL might die! Ugh.\n\nWait let me recompute L for my profile shape: moduleContext = join of ALL string literals in the module: 2000 functions × 2 literals ('field_i' ~10B, docstring? my profile test had docstrings? The profile template: docstring \"Handler %d: read the request...\" (~50B) + \"field_%d\" (~10B) + \"\" (empty, skipped?) + \"field_%d\" key (~10B) + \"size_%d\" (~10B) ≈ 90B per function → L ≈ 180KB. My gen_dense corpus: ~7 literals ≈ 150B → L ≈ 300KB.\n\nWith (iii): per-file fresh str_args ≈ F×4×(2B+C) + F×1×L = 2000×4×2.4KB + 2000×300KB = 19MB + 600MB = 620MB/file... The end-call joins still copy L per function. F·L = quadratic in F remains (one copy per function instead of four). 2000×300KB=600MB per file, ×3 files — partitioned per file → each partition ≈ 620MB + graph structure ≈ fine under 3.3GB? Probably SURVIVES. But it's still quadratic — at 5000 functions/file it dies again (5000×~300KB=1.5GB + ...). Hmm.\n\nThe tokens term has the same shape: the previous fix made tokens ride as ONE joined Const on end — join cost F·L_tok (13KB×F=26MB/file at 2000) — quadratic in F but with a small constant (512-token cap bounds L_tok!). The tokens are CAPPED, so the quadratic term is bounded: F·(capped 512 tokens ≈ ≤16KB?) → 2000×16KB=32MB. Acceptable.\n\nSo for literals, the SYMMETRIC complete fix: cap moduleContext like moduleTokens (bounded L), AND carry it on end only? Or just cap? Capping alone: L→≤64KB (or whatever), stays on 4 calls → 4F·64KB = 512MB at F=2000 — meh. Cap + end-only: F·64KB = 128MB at F=2000. \n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider what's actually SEMANTICALLY DEFENSIBLE rather than what's merely cheap:\n\nThe module literal context exists so a function-scoped question (\"does this function's module mention X literal\") can be answered at function scope. Re-carriage = the mechanism. The CAP question: is an unbounded module literal context even coherent? pyContextBaseTokenLimit=512 bounds the structured token re-carriage. The literal context SHOULD have the same bound for the same reason. What bound? The natural mirror: cap the number of literal tokens at pyContextBaseTokenLimit (512). openrazer's mouse.py: 1910 literals → first 512 kept. Does any finding in openrazer depend on literals 513-1910 riding on FUNCTION context calls? Measurable.\n\nBut wait — 512 literals can still be MBs (giant literals). Cap BYTES too? A byte cap is a second new constant... Let me look at what pyContextCompact does — maybe there's an existing bounded-compaction helper whose constant I can reuse... Let me read pyContextCompact and pyContextCompactUnbounded.","signature":"e364b1b238b5434ea6eecc19"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"48103be8-7333-4f63-b61f-df5be29bdbcd","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:28:16.201Z"}
