on openrazer directly.\n\nOption B (carry moduleContext on the end call only, like module tokens):\n- ALL literals preserved. The .end call's str_args unchanged (moduleContext stays). The OTHER THREE calls (source/context/sink) lose the module-literal substring from their str_args.\n- Bindings anchored on .source/.context/.sink with val needles that match only module literals (not the function's own body/args) would break. Do any exist? The Python bindings' val needles — e.g. authorization.vyql's contains \"module_call_path:...\" — those are TOKEN needles matching the joined module-token literal (which rides END-ONLY already!). Wait — that's interesting: the tokens ride end-only, and bindings write `args.any.literal contains \"module_call_path:base64.b64encode\"`. Where is that predicate evaluated? If it's evaluated against the function's context calls generally (any of the 5), then end-only carriage works for it BECAUSE the predicate is anchored on the END call or evaluated over args.any including end... Let me check what call these predicates anchor on in authorization.vyql — that tells me which of the 5 calls the bindings actually query!\n\nLet me look at authorization.vyql around line 152.","signature":"8c1aff510028480195cacc12"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"4822ff3a-1416-4045-9714-29c9efb894c0","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:29:32.613Z"}
