{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609191331242f8db43af21945e3","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good — the existing tests pin module TOKENS on the end call (splits str_args by \\x00 and requires exact tokens). My change doesn't touch tokens. The raw moduleContext rides end as a Const → its bytes land inside the end call's str_args join. Note: python_context_test.go line 227 splits end str_args by \\x00 and checks exact membership — the raw moduleContext becomes one big \\x00-separated SEGMENT in the join — could it accidentally introduce a spurious exact token? It joins valToks with \\x00: [lang, name, bodyText, compact, classTokens, decorators, fnTokens, delegated, moduleContext, joinedModuleTokens]. The moduleContext SEGMENT contains \"\\n\" inside (literals joined by \\n) — not \\x00 — so it stays one segment. Exact-token assertions unaffected (they check specific tokens exist; an extra big segment doesn't break membership). BUT the assertion `tokens[\"call_path:base64.b64encode\"]` must NOT exist (unprefixed) — moduleContext is raw literal TEXT (not tokens), so no. Good.\n\nLine 380: \"function-local end leaked module or other-function literal\" — local end args unchanged. Good.\n\nNow write my tests. Also delete the scratch profile test.\n\nFor the budget test — let me think about what number to pin. I'll write the test to measure total bytes of str_args across ALL nodes after lowering a module of 400 functions, each function carrying a distinct ~40B literal plus a module-level marker. Budget: assert total < some MiB with the fix comfortably under and base comfortably over. Let me first write with a t.Logf and run both trees to see actual numbers, then pin.\n\nTest source builder (distinct per-function literals AND module-level literals):\n\nconst marker = \"zz_module_literal_marker_zz\"\nsrc: \nMARKER_CONST = \"zz_module_literal_marker_zz\"\ndef handler_i(request):\n    data_i = request.get('field_i_padpadpadpadpadpadpadpad')\n    return data_i\n\nWait — moduleContext includes ALL literals in the module (functions' too): F×40B + marker. At F=400: L≈16KB.\n- base per function: 4 calls × join(L + 2B + C + fnTokens + ...) — B≈50B tiny here. join ≈ L ≈ 16KB → 4×16KB=64KB/function → 400 funcs = 25.6MB.\n- fix: 3×(small ~1KB) + end: L + small ≈ 17KB → ≈ 20KB/function → 8MB.\n\nHmm — but wait: fnTokens per function ≈ small; moduleTokens joined ≈ capped 512 × ~15B = 8KB — rides ONLY end (in both base-after-previous-fix and my fix). So fix end join ≈ 2B + C + fnTokens + L + 8KB ≈ 16+8+1 ≈ 25KB; 3 non-end joins ≈ 3×~1KB. Total/function ≈ 28KB → 11MB. Base: 3 non-end × (L+8KB? no—) wait at BASE (pre-my-fix): moduleTokens join (8KB) rides end only (previous fix), moduleContext (16KB) rides all 4. So base joins/function = 4×16KB + 8KB + ... ≈ 72KB → 29MB. Ratio 2.6×. Pin budget at 16MB: fix 11MB pass, base 29MB fail. OK but margins are meh — let me make function literals longer (80B): L=32KB → base ≈ 4×32=130KB+8KB → 55MB; fix ≈ 33+8+1=42KB→17MB. Pin 24MB. Ratio ~3.2×.\n\nHmm, actually simpler and MUCH more decisive: pin the NON-END context calls' total str_args bytes directly — the economy claim is \"the module's literal mass does not ride the four per-function calls\". Budget test: sum len(str_args) over nodes whose callee_path is one of the three non-end context paths ≤ F × 2KiB (each call carries only the function's own body text/tokens ~1-2KB at most). At base each such call ≈ L+8KB — fails hard. This pins the invariant itself rather than an arbitrary total. And ALSO assert the end calls still carry the marker (facts preserved).\n\nLet me now write both tests into python_context_economy_test.go, delete the profile scratch, run on fix tree, then copy to base tree and verify failure.","signature":"488c90669a504238a96c5aa3"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":nul