{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260919133851d1f7c9bda3424e96","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Progress but not done: memory now fits (2.9GB peak, under the 3.3GiB stop), but the **180s timeout** kills it: rc=124 = timeout. Partition 1/3 took 1m12s for 2 files; partition 2 1m14s for 1 file — ~2.5 minutes per partition × 3 partitions = way over 180s.\n\nSo the memory term is closed but the WALL-CLOCK term isn't: each ~2000-function file takes ~70s to scan. Why so slow? The nolit corpus (no literals): 27.4s total for 3 partitions ≈ 9s/partition. With literals: 70s/partition. The literal mass makes it 8× slower — the str_args joins on the end call are F·L = 2000×300KB = 600MB of joining per file — plus val matching (bindings doing strings.Contains over ~300KB str_args × how many bindings?) — THAT could be the real time sink: value matching iterates bindings over call nodes with big str_args.\n\nHmm wait — but which str_args are big after my fix? The end call's join (L+tokens ≈ 313KB+ per function) — 2000 end calls × 313KB = 626MB joined per file. The valConds evaluation: valContainsLower does lowerString(tokens) — if any uppercase/non-ascii → ToLower allocates ANOTHER 313KB string per check!! And the valueTokenCache caches per node... The bindings' val checks over these 2000 fat end calls: 2000 × (several bindings × 313KB scans) = GBs of scanning. That's the 70s.\n\nSo the remaining problem: the end call's str_args is O(L) per function — the F·L term again, now paid in TIME (and still some memory). My earlier analysis said this: end-only carriage keeps F·L. At F=2000, L=300KB → 600MB/file and heavy scanning. The previous session's corpus survived because their L was ~50KB.\n\nTo close the gap under 4GB AND 180s for realistic dense corpora, F·L on the end call must shrink too. Now the CAP becomes necessary — or a smarter representation.\n\nThink about what val matching needs: substring presence of