o existing constant.\n\nHmm, hold on, let me look at this from the \"what would the file's author do\" angle. The file ALREADY has: pyContextBaseTokenLimit=512 for structured tokens. The literal context collection is `pyModuleLiteralContext` — zero bounds. The natural, symmetric, reviewable change: bound the literal context collection by the SAME token limit — stop after pyContextBaseTokenLimit literals. One line, one existing constant, mirrors the established design. Per-literal length: the existing convention (pyContextValue → compact 160) suggests literals that matter are short; but a 1MB single literal still rides. To also bound THAT: skip literals longer than some bound? There's precedent: pyContextValue drops tokens whose compact exceeds 160. I could apply pyContextValue-ish per-literal bounding... but that CHANGES the text (compaction). Skip-if-too-long is cleaner: a literal whose raw length exceeds N contributes nothing. What N? 160 is the compact convention... but raw literals like SQL templates (legitimately matched?) can be 200+ bytes. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at what actual binding needles exist over raw literal text: search the python bindings for `args.any.literal contains` needles that are LONG (>40 chars) — those would be the ones needing long literal text.","signature":"d048e6e143f54f4a917c01c5"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"2a41ed65-677f-4a1d-81ed-f843643d2fe7","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:39:17.236Z"}
