{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609191339187a41ffbb20fd42f6","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The longest raw needles bindings search for in literal args:\n- 150 chars: an `assignment_flow_reaches_operation:...` token (a structured token, not raw literal text)\n- 83: `assignment_flow_reaches_store:...` (structured token)\n- 54: python_review tokens (structured)\n- Others are short identifiers/call paths.\n\nSo the longest needle over RAW literal text is well under 160 bytes. The existing convention: pyContextCompact bounds a literal's token contribution at 160 compacted bytes. A per-literal bound and a count bound mirroring the token design are defensible.\n\nNow — hmm, wait. Let me stop and reconsider the whole approach once more, because there's something cleaner available.\n\nThe needles are SHORT substrings. The module literal context is a joined blob. Value matching = substring scan. The COST driver: F functions × L bytes joined + scanned. \n\nWhat if the end call carried the module literal context as ONE Const (it does now) — the cost is the JOIN into str_args per end call. What if... the join is the problem — could val matching read Const ARGS instead of str_args? That's the previous session's rejected lowering-side change (35 spec subtests broke) — no.\n\nOK so the cap. Decide the exact shape:\n\n```go\n// pyModuleLiteralContext joins the module's string literals ... bounded the way\n// the structured token walk is bounded: at most pyContextBaseTokenLimit literals,\n// each at most pyContextLiteralMaxBytes raw bytes...\n```\n\nHmm, wait — actually, is a per-literal byte bound needed for the GAP? My dense corpus has ~40B literals; giant literals (100KB) come from generated data tables (uts46data.py: 14k literals — data, not code). The count cap at 512 handles uts46data (keeps first 512). A single giant literal (e.g. a 1MB docstring) — count cap keeps it whole → L=1MB → F×1MB joins — dies again. But is a 1MB-single-literal module a real shape? License headers ~1-2KB. Big SQL templates ~10KB. A 1MB literal is pathological. But 10KB × 512 = 5MB L — pathological too. The count cap alone bounds the common case; adding a per-literal byte cap bounds the rest.\n\nThe existing per-literal convention is 160 (compact, for TOKENS). For RAW literal context, 160 is too tight (SQL template needles could exceed... wait no — needles are ≤150 for structured tokens; raw-text needles: let me check what the longest needle that could match RAW literal text is: the assignment_flow ones are structured tokens (generated by the frontend with prefixes), python_review: same. Raw literal needles: \"password=password\" (17), \"os.path.join\" (12), \"-c\" (2), \"HTTP_PROXY\" (10), \"event_id\", \"is_private\"... The 150/83-char ones are TOKENS that ride as Const VALUES (tokens are literal-ish strings) — they'd still match via the tokens (which stay, capped at 512 count as today).\n\nSo for raw literal text, needles ≤ ~50 chars. A per-literal bound anywhere ≥160 preserves every real needle's matchability.\n\nCleanest symmetric design: in pyModuleLiteralContext, cap collection at pyContextBaseTokenLimit literals AND skip literals longer than... hmm. Introduce `pyModuleLiteralMaxBytes = 512`? New constant. OR reuse the compact convention: only literals whose RAW length ≤ some existing constant? There's no existing raw-length constant.\n\nAlternative aggregate bound: stop adding literals once the joined context reaches N bytes (a byte-budget cap): `pyModuleLiteralContextBudget = 64 << 10` (64KB)? One new constant, bounds BOTH count and total size (giant literal: first literal fills/quits at 64KB... no — a single 1MB literal would blow past before check; check before adding: if len(tok) > remaining budget → stop (or skip-and-continue?). Stop = deterministic prefix).\n\nA byte budget is the most direct bound on the cost driver (L itself): L ≤ 64KB → end join ≤ 64KB+tokens → F×~72KB at F=2000 = 145MB/file, scan time fine. mouse.py: 1910 literals ≈ 30KB total (device names short) — probably UNDER 64KB → NO truncation on openrazer at all!! Let me check: mouse.py literals — average ~15B × 1910 ≈ 29KB < 64KB → untouched. uts46data.py: 14k literals ≈ 300KB → truncated to 64KB (venv/vendored — likely excluded from scans anyway). My dense corpus: L≈300KB → 64KB → F×(64+13)KB ≈ 155MB/file joins. Fine.\n\nSo a byte budget of 64KB:\n- openrazer (the blocked rank's repo): provably NO change (mouse.py L≈29KB — let me measure exactly).\n- BenchmarkPython: max 45 literals/file — untouched.\n- RealVuln: files with L ≤64KB untouched; over → truncated (measure parity).\n- The dense repro: bounded → completes in time.\n\nThe byte budget beats the count cap on parity (real repos keep ALL their literals unless the module's literal mass is truly huge) and bounds the pathologies directly.\n\nImplementation: \n\n```go\n// pyModuleLiteralMaxBytes bounds the module literal context ...\nconst pyModuleLiteralMaxBytes = 64 << 10\n\nfunc (c *pyConv) pyModuleLiteralContext(root *tree_sitter.Node) string {\n\tvar toks []string\n\tvar kept int\n\tvar walk ...\n\t\tif c.kind(n) == \"string\" || c.kind(n) == \"concatenated_string\" {\n\t\t\ttext := c.text(n)\n\t\t\tif kept+len(text) > pyModuleLiteralMaxBytes { STOP ENTIRE WALK }\n\t\t\ttoks = append(toks, text); kept += len(text) + 1\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nStop condition: once kept would exceed, return what's joined (walk can just stop descending — need a flag or early return up the recursion; simplest: check budget at each string node and set a done flag checked at walk entry).\n\nAlso — docstring/comment: py docstrings are string expressions → included. Fine, existing behavior.\n\nWait, one more consideration — determinism: walk order = tree order = source order. Deterministic prefix. Good.\n\nAnd the .end call still carries the (bounded) module context; the module-level analysis.module.context call carries `text` (full source, unchanged — once per file, fine).\n\nTest updates:\n- TestPythonModuleLiteralContextRidesTheEndCallAlone — unchanged (marker at module scope is the FIRST literal → in the prefix).\n- Budget test — add a case pinning the bound: a module with MANY big literals → the moduleContext Const on end calls is ≤ pyModuleLiteralMaxBytes (+ the joined tokens stay). And that the end str_args no longer join ~600MB... the existing budget test (2.5MiB over 1200 calls) already pins the non-end side. Add TestPythonModuleLiteralContextIsBounded: module with 200 literals of 1KB each (200KB > 64KB) → assert: the end call's moduleContext Const length ≤ 64KB+1 and the first literal's marker present, and a literal beyond the bound absent. At base: moduleContext = 200KB, no bound → FAIL. \n\nThen re-measure dense3/densepy through the wrapper (time + memory), then the full parity program.\n\nHmm — also one question: should the bound apply the same on the previous path (nolit has ~no literals — unaffected). Yes, uniform.\n\nNow, about mouse.py's exact L: let me measure the actual joined size for openrazer's files to confirm < 64KB, and check RealVuln files' L distribution to predict parity impact.","signature":"569d10f853c044f38c1de4dc"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"89772532-2b5e-494a-bbe3-fb0eb6f6ca5e","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:39:51.008Z"}
