{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609191346139deed6b238a94735","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The campaign's heaviest Python corpus by far: `realvuln-owasp-web-playground` — 20,109 functions, ~5MB literal mass, 1388 files (mostly venv: pip, rich, pygments vendored — the emoji/lexer tables I saw). Whether its venv is excluded matters (\"tree-walk skips vendored dependency directories\" gap was FIXED — so venv/site-packages probably IS excluded from scans).\n\nThe key structural difference from my synthetic: 20k functions spread over 1388 files ≈ 14 functions/file average — NOT 2000/file. The per-function re-carriage cost scales with the MODULE's literal mass, and real modules are small. My synthetic (2000 funcs/file) is the adversarial shape — realistic repos don't have 2000-function single modules... but openrazer's mouse.py has how many functions? Let me check: mouse.py 1910 literals — functions? Probably ~50-100 (device capability matrices with literal-heavy dicts).\n\nSo the pathological dimension is functions-per-file × literal-mass-per-file. Real files: mouse.py = ~55KB × ~80 functions ≈ 4.4MB end-join mass — fine. My synthetic: 300KB × 2000.\n\nNow the pragmatic decision on the budget and the timeout:\n- 64KB budget + end-only: dense3 (6000 funcs, 3 files) completes at 67s. densepy (24k funcs, 12 files) at ~264s — over 180s.\n\nIs densepy's shape within the gap's demand? The gap says \"under the mandated 4GB cap and 180 second timeout the repository scan writes an empty report\" — the repro needs SOME corpus that (a) at base writes the empty report, (b) at fix completes. dense3 now does exactly that. densepy at fix improves from \"dies at memory stop, empty report\" (base: rc=1 3.4GB) to \"rc=124 timeout with partial partitions\" — hmm, still no complete report at fix.\n\nBut wait — should I push further? Where does the remaining 13s/file go? If it's the val-matcher scanning the 77KB end str_args per function per binding-with-needles, there may be a cheap engine-side win with NO semantic change. Let me profile properly — use Go pprof on a single-file scan. Does the binary have a profile flag? Let me check main.go flags quickly... Actually quicker: perf? Not installed maybe. Simplest: GODEBUG=gctrace=1 to see if it's GC-bound, plus a quick timing breakdown by running the scan with -v? Hmm.\n\nActually — let me reason about the biggest remaining per-file cost: 2000 end calls × (64KB literal blob + 13KB tokens) join = 154MB of joins (allocation + copy ≈ maybe 1-2s), then the DIRECT val path: valCondsDirectForNodeCached → nodeDirectValueTokens(n) — what does that return? Probably str_args or joined arg values. Then valCondsFoldedNeedles scans with fold-on-the-fly (no allocation unless non-ascii/uppercase — my literals are lowercase x's; folding scans are allocation-free but still O(len) per needle). How many bindings have ValMatches needles AND get evaluated per analysis end call? Only bindings whose shapes match analysis.function.context.end path... maybe dozens. 2000 calls × dozens × 77KB = GBs of scanning → seconds. Plus lowering itself (563k nodes).\n\nOptions to cut densepy to <180s without shrinking the budget:\n1. **Cache the folded scan per (str_args, needle)?** — the same 77KB scanned for the SAME needle across 2000 DIFFERENT strings (each function's end str_args differs: name/bodyText). No reuse.\n\n2. **Don't join the literal blob into every end call's str_args — leave it only as the Const arg.** Value matching on str_args loses it... BUT — look at `valCondsForSinkDirectSegments` and `directSegments`: they read `call.Prop(\"str_args\")` PLUS per-arg nodes' str_args (`addArg` walks ArgPropKey → arg node → arg's str_args)! For the DIRECT SINK path (analysis.function.context.* — see `valCondsForSinkCached`: `strings.HasPrefix(call.Prop(\"callee_path\"), \"analysis.function.context.\")` → direct segments = call str_args + each arg NODE's str_args). The Const NODE for moduleContext carries StrArgs = the blob (shared backing, cheap!). So IF the matcher assembles from arg nodes, the blob need NOT be in the call's str_args join — the segment walk picks it up from the Const node!\n\nWhoa — wait. But `valCondsDirectForNodeCached` (the non-sink direct path) reads `nodeDirectValueTokens(n)` — need to check what that is. If it also unions arg nodes' values, then the str_args join doesn't need the blob for THESE analysis calls, and the join could skip giant literals... but the join is generic lowering (all calls), and changing WHICH literals join into analysis calls' str_args = the previous session's rejected approach... no wait, they rejected SKIPPING THE CONST+ARG NODES (presence broke). Keeping nodes but slimming the join is different: value matching would still see values via arg-node segments IF the matcher reads segments.\n\nHmm, but `valCondsForNode` (line 348) reads direct := n.Prop(\"str_args\") — the call's OWN str_args only. And valCondsFoldedNeedles(nodeDirectValueTokens(n), ...) — let me read nodeDirectValueTokens. If some paths read only call.str_args, slimming the join breaks them.\n\nThis is getting deep. Time check: I've spent a lot of session budget. Let me re-center on what the task needs:\n\n1. The PR must close the gap with a failing test ✓ (dense3: base dies with empty report; fix completes with report, under 4GB/180s).\n2. Parity must hold on campaign corpora.\n3. densepy (24k funcs in 12 files): at base rc=1 empty report; at fix rc=124 timeout. Better than base but not complete. Is that acceptable? The gap's behaviour is about THE blocked rank's repo-level proof (openrazer — fine at 941MB/40s) and whole-repo scans that die. My dense3 demonstrates closed. densepy demonstrates... an even denser corpus still times out. Hmm — but honestly, 24k same-shaped handlers in 12 files is beyond any real repository's density (heaviest real corpus: owasp-web-playground 20k funcs over 1388 files — 14/file not 2000/file). The per-MODULE quadratic is what matters for realism: real modules are small. My 64KB bound + end-only carriage makes cost per module bounded by (functions × 77KB) — for real modules (≤100 funcs) trivial.\n\nLet me check owasp-web-playground excluding venv — its non-venv files: how many funcs/literals? That's the realistic heaviest. And then VERIFY it completes under the mandated wrapper at fix (and check base on it too — it's part of the 66-repo parity sweep anyway).\n\nActually, let me reconsider the previous session's densepy claim once more: \"fix rc=0, peak 2,743,744 kB, 4 partitions x 40s, report written\" — 12 files in 4 partitions?! With THEIR corpus, PlanPartitions grouped 12 files into 4 partitions (3 files each, 40s each = 160s — squeaked under 180s). With MY heavier corpus the planner made 12 partitions (1-2 files each × 22s). Both ~160-270s. The margin was luck-of-the-template. This confirms: the previous attempt did NOT actually leave comfortable margin; my rework brings real margin to dense3-scale and improves densepy from memory-death to timeout-at-9-of-12-partitions.\n\nDecision: can I cheaply win the remaining time? One more idea with ZERO semantic change — the 64KB blob is joined into str_args ONCE per function... what if lowering skipped re-joining UNCHANGED huge literal args — no, semantics of the join = what val matching reads.\n\nAlternatively reduce partitions' overhead: 12 sequential partitions each pay full rule-compile/setup? Each partition 22s for 1-2 files; nolit showed 9s/file inherent. Hmm — the per-partition fixed overhead is small.\n\nWhat about the LITERAL MASS time driver = α≈0.2s/KB/file... wait let me re-derive: dense3 file (64KB budget): 22s; nolit file: 9s → 13s for 64KB → 0.2s/KB. That's SLOW for 154MB of joins+scans... something superlinear per file maybe (the valueTokenCache maps growing with 2000 fat entries? sinkValueRaw caching 77KB strings per call → cache itself holds 154MB!). The CACHE holds the raw strings — memory 1.7GB ok. Time: GC churn over ~300MB allocations per file...\n\nOK here's the thing — I should stop optimizing the adversarial synthetic. Realistic corpus = owasp-web-playground (the campaign's heaviest Python repo, 20k funcs/1388 files/5MB literals). If THAT completes under 180s at fix (and dies or not at base), the gap is closed for every corpus the campaign measures. Let me run it through the wrapper, base and fix, and also check openrazer timings. Then the parity sweep.","signature":"d23cf678362741e7b7fc4f82"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d3b39ce-4baf-4531-a837-11deefe45eb7","uuid":"d0bcfae1-962e-40d0-b06a-f3daeeb1b8d8","timestamp":"2026-09-19T05:46:53.261Z"}
